1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,080
The entire history of California is sitting in your room right now.

2
00:00:04,080 --> 00:00:07,600
The jeans you're wearing, the phone in your hand, the app you're watching this on,

3
00:00:07,600 --> 00:00:10,880
the movies you grew up with, even the almonds in your kitchen.

4
00:00:11,440 --> 00:00:16,560
All of it came from one strip of coast, a coast named after an island that never existed.

5
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,040
No really, the name comes from a Spanish adventure novel from 1510

6
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:24,080
about an island of warrior women where the only metal was gold.

7
00:00:25,440 --> 00:00:29,760
And for more than 100 years, real map makers drew California as an island.

8
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:34,240
A king of Spain finally had to announce it officially, California is not an island.

9
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:38,160
Today, that not-island has one of the five biggest economies on Earth.

10
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:44,080
But the man who found California's gold died poor, so did the man who owned the land,

11
00:00:44,080 --> 00:00:46,080
so did the first millionaire the gold ever made.

12
00:00:47,200 --> 00:00:48,480
So who actually got rich?

13
00:00:49,280 --> 00:00:54,080
Let's go back 13,000 years. Long before the name, people were already here.

14
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,360
By the time Europe showed up, there were around 300,000 of them,

15
00:00:57,440 --> 00:00:59,200
speaking close to 100 different languages.

16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,680
A hundred! In one state.

17
00:01:02,400 --> 00:01:05,920
No kings, no empire, just hundreds of small nations.

18
00:01:06,560 --> 00:01:10,240
On the coast, the Chumash built plank canoes and paddled out to the islands.

19
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:12,720
In the oak valleys, families ground acorns into flour,

20
00:01:13,280 --> 00:01:16,320
and they lit small, careful fires to keep the forest healthy.

21
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,520
Hold on to those fires. California is going to need them.

22
00:01:20,320 --> 00:01:23,520
And the gold? It was lying right there in the riverbeds.

23
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:27,680
For 13,000 years, people stepped over it, and nobody rushed anywhere.

24
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:30,560
That was about to change.

25
00:01:30,560 --> 00:01:34,560
1542. A captain named Cabrillo sails up the coast for Spain.

26
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:37,040
He's the first European to see it.

27
00:01:37,840 --> 00:01:38,960
And Spain's reaction?

28
00:01:39,840 --> 00:01:41,200
Nice. We'll get back to you.

29
00:01:42,240 --> 00:01:42,640
They did.

30
00:01:43,440 --> 00:01:45,040
227 years later.

31
00:01:46,160 --> 00:01:50,400
In between, the English sea captain Francis Drake stopped by in 1579.

32
00:01:50,880 --> 00:01:54,560
He fixed his ship, claimed the whole place for his queen, and left.

33
00:01:56,000 --> 00:01:57,680
So what finally woke Spain up?

34
00:01:58,400 --> 00:01:58,720
Russia.

35
00:01:59,520 --> 00:02:02,160
Russian fur hunters were creeping down from Alaska,

36
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:04,800
and suddenly that quiet coast looked worth keeping.

37
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:09,040
So in 1769, soldiers and priests marched north.

38
00:02:09,760 --> 00:02:12,480
Over the next 54 years, they built 21 missions,

39
00:02:12,480 --> 00:02:17,680
from San Diego to Sonoma, linked by one road. El Camino Real, the Royal Road.

40
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:19,760
San Diego.

41
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:22,240
Santa Barbara. San Francisco.

42
00:02:22,880 --> 00:02:24,880
Every one of those cities began as a mission.

43
00:02:25,680 --> 00:02:29,440
You have been reading Spain's to-do list on road signs your whole life.

44
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:32,640
But inside the mission walls, it was a different story.

45
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:35,680
Native families were brought in to work and to pray.

46
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:40,480
Many were not free to leave, and the diseases that came with the newcomers

47
00:02:40,480 --> 00:02:41,760
moved faster than any priest.

48
00:02:43,280 --> 00:02:44,480
Tens of thousands died.

49
00:02:46,160 --> 00:02:48,960
Rush number one was for souls and for empire.

50
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:50,880
It would not be the last.

51
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:55,200
1821, Mexico wins its independence from Spain,

52
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,520
and California changes flags without a battle.

53
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:02,000
Mexico closes the missions and hands out the land as giant ranches.

54
00:03:02,640 --> 00:03:04,000
The business is cattle.

55
00:03:04,000 --> 00:03:07,680
Cow hides were worth so much that sailors called them California banknotes.

56
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:10,800
Then the Americans began to trickle in.

57
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:15,360
First by ship, then in 1841 by wagon, straight over the mountains.

58
00:03:16,240 --> 00:03:21,280
And in June of 1846, about 30 of them marched into the little town of Sonoma,

59
00:03:21,280 --> 00:03:25,360
arrested a Mexican general in his own house, and declared a brand new country.

60
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:26,560
The California Republic.

61
00:03:27,760 --> 00:03:31,440
They needed a flag, so they painted a grizzly bear on a piece of cotton.

62
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:32,720
People said it looked more like a pig.

63
00:03:34,000 --> 00:03:35,760
The Republic lasted 25 days.

64
00:03:36,400 --> 00:03:39,600
Then the United States Navy arrived and said, thanks, we'll take it from here.

65
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,120
By the way, that bear is still on the state flag.

66
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:46,080
And the California grizzly? Extinct.

67
00:03:46,080 --> 00:03:47,840
The last one was shot in 1922.

68
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:52,240
Now watch the dates, because this is the worst timing in history.

69
00:03:53,280 --> 00:03:58,720
February 2nd, 1848, Mexico signs a treaty and hands California to the United States.

70
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:03,120
Nine days earlier, on a river in the California hills,

71
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:05,360
a carpenter had looked down into the water.

72
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,000
January 24th, 1848.

73
00:04:08,560 --> 00:04:12,240
James Marshall is building a sawmill for his boss, John Sutter.

74
00:04:12,320 --> 00:04:14,080
In the water, something shines.

75
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:15,120
Gold.

76
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:16,720
Remember the novel?

77
00:04:16,720 --> 00:04:18,400
The island where the only metal was gold?

78
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:22,480
338 years later, the made-up story came true.

79
00:04:23,200 --> 00:04:24,880
Sutter begged everyone to keep it secret.

80
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:28,160
It stayed secret for about five minutes.

81
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:31,040
Because a shopkeeper named Sam Brannan heard the news,

82
00:04:31,040 --> 00:04:34,960
and before he told a single person, he quietly bought up every pick,

83
00:04:34,960 --> 00:04:36,480
pan, and shovel he could find.

84
00:04:37,120 --> 00:04:40,080
Then, the story goes, he ran through the streets of San Francisco,

85
00:04:40,080 --> 00:04:42,000
waving a bottle of gold dust and shouting,

86
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:43,760
Gold! Gold from the American River!

87
00:04:44,800 --> 00:04:46,720
San Francisco had about 1,000 people.

88
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:49,280
Two years later, it had 25,000.

89
00:04:49,840 --> 00:04:52,560
Sailors jumped off their ships and ran for the hills.

90
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,360
Hundreds of empty ships sat rotting in the bay.

91
00:04:55,360 --> 00:04:57,600
Some were dragged ashore and turned into hotels.

92
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:00,000
They came from everywhere.

93
00:05:00,000 --> 00:05:03,360
China, Chile, Mexico, Australia, France,

94
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:06,480
around 300,000 people in seven years.

95
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:08,240
An egg could cost a dollar.

96
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:10,000
A shovel could cost a small fortune.

97
00:05:10,800 --> 00:05:13,200
California skipped the waiting line completely.

98
00:05:13,200 --> 00:05:15,840
In 1850, it became the 31st state.

99
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:17,040
But the hills were not empty.

100
00:05:17,680 --> 00:05:21,680
Around 150,000 Native people lived in California when the gold was found.

101
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:26,160
By 1870, about 30,000 were left.

102
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:31,440
Disease, hunger, and killing that the new state paid for.

103
00:05:32,560 --> 00:05:36,080
Its first governor told lawmakers to expect a war of extermination.

104
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:41,040
In 2019, the governor of California stood in front of tribal leaders

105
00:05:41,040 --> 00:05:42,000
and called it what it was.

106
00:05:42,880 --> 00:05:43,520
A genocide.

107
00:05:44,400 --> 00:05:45,280
And he apologized.

108
00:05:47,120 --> 00:05:48,320
And the men who started it all?

109
00:05:49,120 --> 00:05:50,640
Sutter's workers ran off to dig.

110
00:05:51,200 --> 00:05:52,480
Strangers took over his land.

111
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:54,960
He died still asking Congress for help.

112
00:05:56,160 --> 00:05:58,240
Marshall, the man who saw the gold first,

113
00:05:58,800 --> 00:06:00,560
ended up selling his own autograph to get by.

114
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:04,240
So if the finder didn't get rich,

115
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:05,680
and the landowner didn't get rich,

116
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:07,280
who did?

117
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:09,840
Remember the shopkeeper.

118
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:11,600
We'll come back to him.

119
00:06:11,600 --> 00:06:14,080
But first, everyone had the same problem.

120
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:14,960
Getting there.

121
00:06:14,960 --> 00:06:16,960
By wagon, it took four to six months.

122
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,200
By ship around the bottom of South America,

123
00:06:19,200 --> 00:06:20,320
about the same.

124
00:06:20,320 --> 00:06:21,600
People died doing both.

125
00:06:22,320 --> 00:06:25,040
So America decided to do something crazy.

126
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:27,920
Build a railroad across the entire continent.

127
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:30,880
On the California side, the bosses were four men from Sacramento.

128
00:06:31,440 --> 00:06:32,880
Four shopkeepers.

129
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:35,920
They had made their money selling supplies to gold miners.

130
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:37,040
Starting to see a pattern?

131
00:06:37,840 --> 00:06:40,480
The men who actually built it were mostly Chinese.

132
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:44,160
At the peak, about nine out of every ten workers on the western half.

133
00:06:44,160 --> 00:06:48,240
More than 12,000 men cutting through solid mountain granite

134
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:50,720
through winters with snow deeper than a house

135
00:06:50,720 --> 00:06:52,720
for less pay than the white workers beside them.

136
00:06:53,680 --> 00:06:55,440
May 10th, 1869.

137
00:06:55,440 --> 00:06:56,560
The two tracks meet.

138
00:06:57,120 --> 00:06:59,120
Six months becomes one week.

139
00:06:59,840 --> 00:07:00,400
And the thanks?

140
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:03,440
In 1882, the United States passed a law

141
00:07:03,440 --> 00:07:05,840
banning Chinese workers from entering the country.

142
00:07:06,560 --> 00:07:09,680
It was the first time America shut its door on one nationality.

143
00:07:10,480 --> 00:07:13,360
One of those four shopkeepers was named Leland Stanford.

144
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:15,520
He spent part of his fortune on a new university.

145
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:17,760
Built on his horse farm south of San Francisco.

146
00:07:18,400 --> 00:07:19,520
Remember that farm.

147
00:07:19,520 --> 00:07:20,560
It is going to matter.

148
00:07:21,420 --> 00:07:23,660
Now the trains needed passengers.

149
00:07:23,660 --> 00:07:25,500
So the railroads did something new.

150
00:07:25,500 --> 00:07:27,180
They advertised a place.

151
00:07:27,980 --> 00:07:28,940
Posters.

152
00:07:28,940 --> 00:07:31,180
Orange crates painted like paradise.

153
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:32,540
Come to California.

154
00:07:32,540 --> 00:07:33,900
Where it is summer all year.

155
00:07:34,940 --> 00:07:36,220
During one price war,

156
00:07:36,220 --> 00:07:39,660
a ticket from Kansas City to Los Angeles dropped to one dollar.

157
00:07:41,020 --> 00:07:44,300
In 1870, Los Angeles had under 6,000 people.

158
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:46,620
By 1930, it had over a million.

159
00:07:47,340 --> 00:07:51,020
Up north, San Francisco learned what the ground could do.

160
00:07:51,020 --> 00:07:53,500
April 18th, 1906.

161
00:07:53,500 --> 00:07:54,780
An earthquake.

162
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:56,380
Then three days of fire.

163
00:07:56,380 --> 00:07:58,460
About 3,000 dead.

164
00:07:58,460 --> 00:08:00,140
Most of the city gone.

165
00:08:00,140 --> 00:08:02,700
They rebuilt it in under 10 years.

166
00:08:02,700 --> 00:08:06,060
Down south, Los Angeles had a different problem.

167
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:09,180
A million people were coming to a place with almost no water.

168
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:12,940
So the city's water chief, William Mulholland, built an aqueduct.

169
00:08:12,940 --> 00:08:15,180
233 miles long.

170
00:08:15,740 --> 00:08:18,300
It reached into a farming valley called Owens Valley

171
00:08:18,300 --> 00:08:19,260
and drank the river.

172
00:08:20,380 --> 00:08:22,220
When the water arrived in 1913,

173
00:08:22,220 --> 00:08:24,380
Mulholland gave a speech. Five words.

174
00:08:25,100 --> 00:08:25,580
There it is.

175
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:26,620
Take it.

176
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:30,780
Owens Lake dried into a bowl of dust.

177
00:08:31,420 --> 00:08:36,140
And a small group of insiders, who had quietly bought the empty land where the water would arrive?

178
00:08:36,140 --> 00:08:38,060
They got very, very rich.

179
00:08:39,340 --> 00:08:42,380
Around the same time, some people with cameras showed up.

180
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:44,700
They wanted sunshine almost every day,

181
00:08:44,700 --> 00:08:47,500
with mountains, desert, and ocean all a short drive apart.

182
00:08:48,220 --> 00:08:49,660
They set up in a quiet suburb.

183
00:08:50,380 --> 00:08:51,340
It was called Hollywood.

184
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:54,060
New rush. Same rule.

185
00:08:55,020 --> 00:08:57,660
And the next crowd arrived with nothing left to lose.

186
00:08:58,300 --> 00:08:59,340
The 1930s.

187
00:08:59,980 --> 00:09:01,980
The middle of America turns to dust.

188
00:09:02,700 --> 00:09:03,740
Farms blow away.

189
00:09:04,460 --> 00:09:09,660
More than 300,000 people load everything onto old cars and drive west,

190
00:09:09,660 --> 00:09:10,940
to the place from the posters.

191
00:09:13,260 --> 00:09:15,740
California did not roll out a welcome mat.

192
00:09:16,460 --> 00:09:22,220
In 1936, Los Angeles sent its own police to the state border to turn poor families around.

193
00:09:24,460 --> 00:09:27,740
The state built by newcomers tried to close the door.

194
00:09:28,540 --> 00:09:31,100
Those who got in picked fruit for pennies,

195
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:33,420
on land owned by somebody else.

196
00:09:35,020 --> 00:09:39,580
Then in 1937, an impossible bridge opened across the Golden Gate.

197
00:09:40,460 --> 00:09:43,020
And four years later, everything changed again.

198
00:09:44,140 --> 00:09:44,460
War.

199
00:09:45,340 --> 00:09:48,300
California became America's factory on the Pacific.

200
00:09:48,300 --> 00:09:50,460
Planes in the south, ships in the north.

201
00:09:51,260 --> 00:09:55,740
The shipyards of one town near San Francisco built 747 ships.

202
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:58,620
One of them was put together in under five days.

203
00:09:59,900 --> 00:10:01,020
Millions came for the work.

204
00:10:01,580 --> 00:10:07,340
Between 1940 and 1950, the population jumped from under 7 million to over 10.

205
00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:12,780
But in 1942, the government forced about 120,000 people of Japanese descent out of their homes

206
00:10:12,780 --> 00:10:15,260
on the West Coast and into camps behind barbed wire.

207
00:10:16,140 --> 00:10:17,900
Two out of three were American citizens.

208
00:10:18,540 --> 00:10:20,700
They lost farms, shops, and homes.

209
00:10:21,340 --> 00:10:23,660
The official apology came 46 years later.

210
00:10:25,820 --> 00:10:29,020
After the war, the soldiers who had passed through remembered the weather.

211
00:10:29,020 --> 00:10:30,140
They came back with families.

212
00:10:30,780 --> 00:10:31,260
Freeways.

213
00:10:31,980 --> 00:10:32,380
Suburbs.

214
00:10:33,020 --> 00:10:34,460
A theme park in an orange grove.

215
00:10:35,180 --> 00:10:39,180
By the early 1960s, California had more people than any other state.

216
00:10:39,180 --> 00:10:40,780
Remember Stanford's farm?

217
00:10:40,780 --> 00:10:44,460
By the 1930s, the valley around it was famous for one thing.

218
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:45,340
Fruit.

219
00:10:45,980 --> 00:10:48,620
Plums, cherries, apricots.

220
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:51,100
People called it the Valley of Heart's Delight.

221
00:10:52,540 --> 00:10:55,660
Then two Stanford graduates started a company in a garage.

222
00:10:56,300 --> 00:10:57,660
Hewlett and Packard.

223
00:10:58,460 --> 00:11:02,300
In 1956, one of the inventors of the transistor moved to the valley,

224
00:11:02,380 --> 00:11:05,500
to be near his mother. He hired eight brilliant young engineers.

225
00:11:06,220 --> 00:11:09,180
He was such a difficult boss that all eight quit together.

226
00:11:09,420 --> 00:11:10,540
They started their own company.

227
00:11:11,100 --> 00:11:12,860
Then their people left and started more.

228
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:14,460
One of those was Intel.

229
00:11:15,260 --> 00:11:18,380
The chips were made of silicon, which comes from sand.

230
00:11:18,380 --> 00:11:20,620
Somebody called the place Silicon Valley.

231
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:22,620
And the fruit trees never came back.

232
00:11:23,820 --> 00:11:25,820
October 1969.

233
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:31,100
A computer in Los Angeles sends the first message on the network that will become the internet.

234
00:11:31,660 --> 00:11:33,740
The message was supposed to be the word login.

235
00:11:34,620 --> 00:11:36,540
The system crashed after two letters.

236
00:11:36,540 --> 00:11:39,260
So the first word ever sent online was... lo.

237
00:11:40,300 --> 00:11:41,180
1976.

238
00:11:41,740 --> 00:11:44,300
Two friends named Steve, and another garage.

239
00:11:45,020 --> 00:11:45,340
Apple.

240
00:11:46,220 --> 00:11:47,420
1998.

241
00:11:47,420 --> 00:11:49,260
Two students, and another garage.

242
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:50,460
Google.

243
00:11:51,180 --> 00:11:52,780
California had found gold again.

244
00:11:53,340 --> 00:11:55,820
This time, it was made of sand.

245
00:11:55,820 --> 00:11:57,660
Add up every one of those rushes.

246
00:11:57,740 --> 00:12:01,180
And today about one in every nine Americans lives in California.

247
00:12:02,620 --> 00:12:03,820
And the bill has arrived.

248
00:12:04,380 --> 00:12:07,340
A typical home costs about twice the American average.

249
00:12:07,340 --> 00:12:10,220
More people sleep on the street here than in any other state.

250
00:12:10,780 --> 00:12:12,540
And the fires keep getting bigger.

251
00:12:12,780 --> 00:12:16,300
In 2018, a single fire erased a town called Paradise.

252
00:12:16,300 --> 00:12:22,300
In January 2025, fires burned into Los Angeles itself and took more than 16,000 buildings.

253
00:12:23,260 --> 00:12:25,580
Remember the small, careful fires from the very beginning?

254
00:12:26,220 --> 00:12:29,500
California is now working with the tribes to bring those fires back.

255
00:12:30,620 --> 00:12:32,060
And there is a new rush.

256
00:12:32,060 --> 00:12:33,660
Artificial intelligence.

257
00:12:33,660 --> 00:12:37,020
Once again, the world is running to San Francisco.

258
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:39,260
So. The question I promised you at the start.

259
00:12:40,140 --> 00:12:42,220
Who actually gets rich in California?

260
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:44,700
Not the man who found the gold.

261
00:12:44,700 --> 00:12:45,820
Not the man who owned the land.

262
00:12:46,700 --> 00:12:51,660
Sam Brannan, the shopkeeper with the shovels? He became California's first millionaire.

263
00:12:52,140 --> 00:12:55,580
Then he lost it all and died with almost nothing.

264
00:12:56,540 --> 00:12:58,700
The railroad? Four shopkeepers.

265
00:12:59,340 --> 00:13:01,740
The water? The men who bought the land first.

266
00:13:02,940 --> 00:13:04,060
And one more shopkeeper.

267
00:13:04,700 --> 00:13:07,420
He arrived in San Francisco in 1853.

268
00:13:08,220 --> 00:13:10,060
He never dug for gold.

269
00:13:10,060 --> 00:13:10,620
Not once.

270
00:13:11,180 --> 00:13:11,980
He sold cloth.

271
00:13:12,700 --> 00:13:17,820
Then in 1873, he and a tailor put metal rivets on a pair of work pants.

272
00:13:18,460 --> 00:13:20,620
His name was Levi Strauss.

273
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:27,260
And in today's rush? One of the most valuable companies on Earth is not digging for AI gold at all.

274
00:13:27,260 --> 00:13:30,380
It sits in Santa Clara and it sells the chips.

275
00:13:31,580 --> 00:13:32,140
The shovels.

276
00:13:33,420 --> 00:13:34,300
The gold ran out.

277
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:35,900
The grizzly is gone.

278
00:13:36,540 --> 00:13:38,060
The island never existed.

279
00:13:39,020 --> 00:13:39,740
But look down.

280
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:42,060
The jeans are still here.

281
00:13:42,860 --> 00:13:45,020
That is the entire history of California.

282
00:13:45,740 --> 00:13:46,940
Everybody comes for the gold.

283
00:13:47,740 --> 00:13:49,660
The smart ones sell the shovels.
